\section{Intermediate languages with different constructors}

In \emph{A Nanopass Framework for Commercial Compiler Development}, Andrew W.\ Keep and R.\ Kent Dybvig describe a framework used to build a Chez Scheme compiler. A \texttt{define-language} specifies terminals, nonterminals, and productions. A derived language can list just the productions and terminals it adds or removes. For example, their early source language allows \texttt{quote} of a datum, while the next restricts it to a smaller class of constants; the associated pass lifts construction of compound data. A backend pass need not recognize every syntax a frontend once did. A seemingly equivalent design with one universal node type can make a removed form invalid only by convention, and invalid forms then travel unnoticed through unrelated passes.

A \texttt{define-pass} declares its input and output languages. Explicit transformer clauses implement changed productions; the framework can generate traversal and reconstruction for compatible unchanged productions. Pattern annotations can combine matching a subterm with calling a transformer on it. That is useful when a pass changes a single operand form deep inside a large tree: the author writes the interesting case, but even unchanged enclosing forms are rebuilt in the output language. Automatic traversal is not a substitute for judgment about execution order. Walking both arms of an input conditional to construct the output is fine; emitting both arms' effects before the generated conditional is not.

\section{Worked pass: make operands atomic}

Jeremy G.\ Siek's \emph{Essentials of Compilation} separates removal of complex operands from explication of control and uses interpreters for the intervening languages. In this teaching variant the source fixes left-to-right evaluation. Let atoms be literals and references to immutable bindings. In the target, calls accept only atomic operators and operands; a complex expression can appear on the right of a single-binding \texttt{let}. A normalizer uses a helper that either returns an atom as is or binds a fresh temporary to a normalized computation and returns the temporary. For example:

\begin{verbatim}
(emit (+ (read-int) 1) (read-int))
  =>
(let ((x (read-int)))
  (let ((y (+ x 1)))
    (let ((z (read-int)))
      (emit y z))))
\end{verbatim}

With input \verb|10 20|, the trace should show two reads in order and then emission of \verb|11 20|. Add an operator expression that reads input before producing a procedure to make sure the helper handles the operator position as well. Atoms in a language with mutation need more care: leaving a mutable variable reference until the final call can delay its load past a later operand's assignment. Restricting atoms to immutable binding references, or first making mutable reads explicit computations, rules out this failure. Likewise, keep the generated bindings for an \texttt{if} arm inside that arm, lest a normalization pass perform I/O that the source would never reach.

\section{Shape checks and semantic checks}

Run an output-language recognizer after the pass to detect illegal nested operands. Separately compare the interpreted result and effect trace for legal terms in the input and output languages. Use binding identities rather than printed spellings so the test can tell normalization failure from a cosmetic choice of temporary names. Exercise wrong arity, failure after the first read, and a conditional whose unused arm would consume the next input. The recognizer checks where calls can occur; none of those behavior checks follows from a valid tree shape alone.

One later choice is whether normalized control should be kept as a tree. In \emph{Administrative Normal Form, Continued}, Luke Maurer, Paul Downen, Zena M.\ Ariola, and Simon Peyton Jones show how join points express shared control in direct style. If a downstream rewrite needs several branches to share a remainder, add a binding for the remainder and a restricted jump form rather than duplicating a large expression or expressing jumps as arbitrary functions. A join can have rules that ordinary functions do not, such as use only as a saturated tail transfer; tree recognizers and binding checks can enforce those separately. The dossier's semantic IR idea (Chapter~\ref{ch:semantic-ir}) names the general principle: make properties visible at the boundary where an optimization depends on them, and distinguish a property checked by syntax from one requiring a semantic argument.

Not every compiler needs that boundary. Erik Hilsdale, J.\ Michael Ashley, R.\ Kent Dybvig, and Daniel P.\ Friedman's \emph{Compiler Construction Using Scheme} describes a teaching compiler that stays in direct style without A-normalizing. Naming every intermediate can make later analysis uniform, but it also creates temporaries that an unsophisticated backend might give needless locations. Their example is a reminder to choose a pass language by the invariants that subsequent passes actually exploit, not by the number of passes it permits.
